    "question": {
      ":question/id": "32795606-a659-5a1b-b028-086ea4b70e79",
      ":question/problem": "Find $j$, if ${a}_{1}-j{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $j$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-j{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-j\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ j\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ j\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $j=2.$",
